  "packageHash": "QEquMYvtjkbUbTR3NaN7prVcJqznibUWhJ4gX7do9pvsPZ/IxRELAr4HhG8Nb+E30gtUvmOpbKk9LZV1p29lYQ==",
  "packageHashAlgorithm": "SHA512",
  "packageSize": 15171337,
  "published": "2026-09-25T13:43:54.793Z",
  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "DID THE RE-TRAINED MODEL ACTUALLY GET BETTER? A re-trained (donor-taken, fine-tuned) model is easy to produce and hard to judge: public benchmarks fall after domain fine-tuning and an LLM judging free text is noisy and circular. This release measures the question instead. A/B AGAINST RECORDED OUTCOMES (TypedDecisions): pair two prediction dumps by (example, question), score both against the labels a business already records as a by-product of operating, and report accuracy, NLL, Brier, ECE/MCE, reliability bins and selective accuracy at each act-threshold side by side, with a paired McNemar exact test, a Wilson interval on the win share and a per-question breakdown; the verdict is BETTER, WORSE, INDISTINGUISHABLE or NO EVIDENCE. fuuga decide compare keeps the existing fidelity gate when called without --data (a conversion must answer identically) and switches to the measured A/B when --data supplies labelled fixtures: new --data, --alpha, --bins, --report, --reference-calibration and --candidate-calibration flags, per-engine temperature tables applied before scoring, JSON or text report. Exit 3 now means a measured regression only; wiring errors (unmatched fixtures, no usable pairs) exit 1, so a CI gate cannot mistake a wrong path for a worse model. TYPED LLM-AS-JUDGE (new JudgeArena module, fuuga decide judge): where no recorded outcome exists, judge two models' free-text answers pairwise as a typed choice over A, B or tie - a probability per option, no free text to parse. Both presentation orders are judged and averaged, so position bias is measured (first-position rate) and a pair whose two orders disagree carries no information and is counted as a tie, never a win. Reports win rate with a Wilson interval and an exact-test p-value, per-category breakdown, act-or-escalate gating (a flipped pair is never confident), an optional rubric pass, a JSONL audit trail, and - when human labels are supplied - the judge's own agreement and calibration against them, so the judge is measured before it is trusted. EXAMPLE: examples/retrain-verification.fsx trains a base model, re-trains a donor copy on a new domain, then verifies the result end to end (fine-tune loss, held-out perplexity on both the new and the original domain to expose forgetting, the decision A/B with McNemar, and the typed judge) offline on CPU in about a minute, and ends with the six CLI commands that reproduce it; new \"Prove a re-trained model is better\" workflow with detours from the fine-tune and evaluate journeys. PREVIOUS (1.0.23) - TYPED DECISIONS (System 1, the Laya / Jev pattern): typed choice, score and noul questions answered as a probability for every named option, zero-shot via option-tag log-probs (Fuuga checkpoint, GGUF, or an OpenAI-compatible endpoint with logprobs) or natively from bilinear decision heads on a bidirectional Fuuga checkpoint (fuuga decide train), temperature scaling fitted per question kind and option-count bucket, calibration metrics, an act-or-escalate gate feeding the minion's escalation verdict, POST /v1/decide in the server, and a DRIFT MONITOR (fuuga drift, --drift-state) that files retraining triggers carrying an approve-or-reject brief and never retrains automatically.",
